.\" Manpage for bssh-keygen
.\" Contact the maintainers to correct errors or typos.
.TH BSSH-KEYGEN 1 "October 2026" "v3.1.0" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
bssh-keygen \- SSH key pair generation tool

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bssh-keygen
[\fB\-t\fR \fItype\fR]
[\fB\-b\fR \fIbits\fR]
[\fB\-f\fR \fIfile\fR]
[\fB\-C\fR \fIcomment\fR]
[\fB\-y\fR]
[\fB\-q\fR]
[\fB\-w\fR \fIprovider\fR]
[\fB\-O\fR \fIoption\fR]
.br
.B bssh-keygen
\fB\-K\fR [\fB\-w\fR \fIprovider\fR] [\fB\-O\fR device=\fIpath\fR]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bssh-keygen
generates SSH key pairs in OpenSSH format. It supports Ed25519 (recommended), RSA, and authenticator-backed ed25519-sk and ecdsa-sk algorithms.
Generated keys are compatible with OpenSSH and can be used for SSH authentication.

By default, bssh-keygen generates an Ed25519 key pair and saves it to
\fI~/.ssh/id_ed25519\fR (private key) and \fI~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub\fR (public key).

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-type " " \fITYPE\fR
Specifies the type of key to generate. Supported types:
.RS
.IP \fBed25519\fR 10
(default, recommended) Ed25519 elliptic curve key. Provides strong security with compact key size
and fast operations.
.IP \fBed25519-sk\fR 10
Ed25519 FIDO security key. Requires a compatible attached authenticator.
.IP \fBecdsa-sk\fR 10
ECDSA P-256 FIDO security key.
.IP \fBrsa\fR 10
RSA key. Use \fB-b\fR to specify key size. Supported for compatibility with legacy systems.
.RE

.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-file " " \fIFILE\fR
Output file path for the private key. The public key is saved to \fIFILE.pub\fR.
Default: \fI~/.ssh/id_<type>\fR (e.g., ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 for Ed25519 keys)

.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bits " " \fIBITS\fR
Number of bits for RSA key. Only applicable when \fB-t rsa\fR is specified.
.RS
.IP "Minimum:" 10
2048 bits
.IP "Maximum:" 10
16384 bits
.IP "Default:" 10
4096 bits
.IP "Recommended:" 10
4096 bits for new deployments
.RE

.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-comment " " \fICOMMENT\fR
Comment to include in the public key. Typically used to identify the key owner.
Default: "bssh-keygen"

Example: \fB-C "user@hostname"\fR

.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-provider " " \fIPROVIDER\fR
Security-key provider. Defaults to \fBinternal\fR, the built-in USB HID provider.
May be a trusted shared library implementing OpenSSH sk-api, or a quoted
\fI$VARIABLE\fR naming an environment variable containing the provider setting.
External providers execute inside the process. Only load trusted libraries.

.TP
.B \-O OPTION
Security-key option; may be repeated with different option names:
.RS
.IP \fBapplication=ssh:NAME\fR 10
Application identifier. Must begin with ssh:. Default: ssh:.
.IP \fBresident\fR 10
Create a discoverable credential that can later be downloaded using \fB-K\fR.
.IP \fBverify-required\fR 10
Require user verification (normally a PIN) when signing.
.IP \fBno-touch-required\fR 10
Do not request user presence for signatures. The authenticator or server may
still require a touch. Use the corresponding server authorized_keys option.
.IP \fBuser=NAME\fR 10
User name for resident credential enrollment.
.IP \fBchallenge=FILE\fR 10
Read enrollment challenge bytes from a regular file (1 byte to 1 MiB).
The default is a random 32-byte challenge.
.IP \fBdevice=PATH\fR 10
Select a specific authenticator device. Also accepted with \fB-K\fR.
.RE
.PP
\fB-O write-attestation=FILE\fR is explicitly unsupported. Unknown, duplicate,
or malformed options fail before authenticator access.

.TP
.BR \-K ", " \-\-download-resident
Prompt for the authenticator PIN and download resident SSH credentials into
the current directory. Filenames follow OpenSSH's id_ed25519_sk_rk or
id_ecdsa_sk_rk naming with application/user suffixes. Unsafe path characters
are escaped. Numeric suffixes distinguish credentials with duplicate or
case-equivalent application/user filenames, including public-key path aliases. Existing key files require
confirmation unless \fB-y\fR is specified. Cannot be combined with \fB-f\fR.

.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Overwrite existing key files without prompting for confirmation.

.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Quiet mode. Suppress all output except error messages.

.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Increase verbosity level. Can be specified multiple times (\fB-vvv\fR) for maximum verbosity.

.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display help message and exit.

.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Display version information and exit.

.SH KEY TYPES
.SS Ed25519 (Recommended)
Ed25519 is a modern elliptic curve signature algorithm that provides:
.IP \(bu 2
128-bit security level (equivalent to RSA-3072)
.IP \(bu 2
Fast key generation and signing operations
.IP \(bu 2
Compact key size (32 bytes public key, 64 bytes private key)
.IP \(bu 2
Deterministic signatures
.IP \(bu 2
Resistance to side-channel attacks
.PP
Ed25519 is recommended for all new key generation.

.SS RSA
RSA is a widely-used public key algorithm. While still secure with sufficient key sizes
(2048+ bits), Ed25519 is preferred due to:
.IP \(bu 2
Faster key generation and operations
.IP \(bu 2
Smaller key sizes for equivalent security
.IP \(bu 2
Better resistance to implementation errors
.PP
RSA support is provided for compatibility with legacy systems.

.SS FIDO Security Keys
The private file stores an opaque handle rather than the authenticator's private
signing key. Keep the file private; the attached authenticator is still required
to authenticate. Defaults are ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_sk and ~/.ssh/id_ecdsa_sk.
Enrollment may prompt for a PIN and require a touch. Resident download always
prompts for a PIN. Device and provider support determines available algorithms
and resident-key operations. Built-in resident enrollment requires a PIN and
CTAP2 credential management (credMgmt or credentialMgmtPreview), used to check
for an existing application/user credential before enrollment. Both resident
and verify-required enrollment require the credProtect extension. Unsupported
authenticators fail explicitly instead of creating a credential with weaker
protection. The current ssh-key encoder supports credential
handles of up to 255 bytes; larger handles fail with an explicit diagnostic.
On Linux, the user needs access to the authenticator's /dev/hidraw device,
normally configured by the FIDO udev rules. The built-in provider supports
Linux and macOS; other platforms require an external provider library.

.SH OUTPUT
.B bssh-keygen
creates two files:

.TP
.I FILE
Private key in OpenSSH format. This file has permissions 0600 (owner read/write only) and should
be kept secret.

.TP
.I FILE.pub
Public key in OpenSSH format. This file can be shared and added to remote servers'
\fIauthorized_keys\fR files.

.PP
Unless \fB-q\fR is specified, bssh-keygen displays:
.IP \(bu 2
Path to the saved private key
.IP \(bu 2
Path to the saved public key
.IP \(bu 2
SHA256 fingerprint of the key
.IP \(bu 2
The public key content

.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Generate Ed25519 Key (Recommended)
.nf
$ bssh-keygen
Your identification has been saved in /home/user/.ssh/id_ed25519
Your public key has been saved in /home/user/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
The key fingerprint is:
SHA256:xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

Public key:
ssh-ed25519 AAAA... bssh-keygen
.fi

.SS Generate Ed25519 Key with Custom Path
.nf
$ bssh-keygen -f ~/.ssh/my_server_key
.fi

.SS Generate Ed25519 Key with Custom Comment
.nf
$ bssh-keygen -C "john@workstation"
.fi

.SS Generate RSA Key with 4096 Bits
.nf
$ bssh-keygen -t rsa -b 4096
.fi

.SS Enroll a Resident Security Key
.nf
$ bssh-keygen -t ed25519-sk -O resident -O application=ssh:work -O user=alice
$ bssh-keygen -t ecdsa-sk -O verify-required -w internal
$ bssh-keygen -K
.fi

.SS Generate Key Non-Interactively
.nf
$ bssh-keygen -f /tmp/deploy_key -C "deploy@server" -y -q
.fi

.SS Generate Key for CI/CD Pipeline
.nf
#!/bin/bash
# Generate a deployment key
bssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ./deploy_key -C "ci-deploy" -y -q

# Display public key for adding to server
cat ./deploy_key.pub
.fi

.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
Default Ed25519 private key.

.TP
.I ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
Default Ed25519 public key.

.TP
.I ~/.ssh/id_rsa
Default RSA private key.

.TP
.I ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub
Default RSA public key.

.TP
.I ~/.ssh/
Default directory for SSH keys. Created with permissions 0700 if it doesn't exist.

.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
Private key files are created with permissions 0600 (owner read/write only).
Never share or expose private key files.
.IP \(bu 2
The ~/.ssh directory is created with permissions 0700 (owner only) if it doesn't exist.
.IP \(bu 2
Ed25519 is recommended over RSA for new keys due to its stronger security properties
and resistance to various attack classes.
.IP \(bu 2
For RSA keys, use at least 2048 bits. 4096 bits is recommended.
.IP \(bu 2
Store private keys securely. Consider using encrypted backup solutions.
.IP \(bu 2
Rotate keys periodically, especially for high-security environments.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful key generation.
.TP
.B 1
Error occurred (invalid options, write failure, etc.)

.SH COMPATIBILITY
Keys generated by bssh-keygen are fully compatible with OpenSSH and can be used with:
.IP \(bu 2
OpenSSH client and server
.IP \(bu 2
bssh client
.IP \(bu 2
bssh-server
.IP \(bu 2
Any SSH implementation supporting OpenSSH key formats

